-- §8 I4 / §3: an interface is identified by its DECLARATION, so `amod.Same` and
-- `zmod.Same` are TWO classes and `foo`'s obligation may only ever be discharged
-- against `zmod.Same`'s impl.
--
-- SPEC ANSWER, HAND-DERIVED BEFORE ANY BINARY WAS RUN: `foo (Box Opaque)` selects
-- `zmod`'s `impl Same (Box a)`, which has no context, so `Opaque` needs nothing —
-- 1. `bar (Box 1)` selects `amod`'s, whose `requires Eq a` grounds to `Eq Int`,
-- which holds — 2. Total 3. ACCEPT.
--
-- MEASURED AS A FALSE REJECT ON THE B-4b-i BASE BINARY (this worktree, its own
-- stdlib/runtime/emitter): `check`/`run`/`build` all exit 1 with
--   `No impl of Eq for Opaque; add 'deriving Eq' to the 'Opaque' type, …`
-- pointed at `foo (Box Opaque)` — a constraint THIS PROGRAM NEVER WROTE, imposed by
-- the OTHER module's class. `findMatchingImplReqsU`'s concrete leg re-minted its query
-- through `ifaceRefBare`, so `ieEntriesForIface` compared a bare SPELLING and reached
-- `amod`'s row first. Its own HEADLESS sibling leg one line below was already
-- identity-keyed (`univHeadless` → `oblIfaceKey`), so the function disagreed with
-- itself.
--
-- ⚠️ THE SWAPPED SIBLING IS HALF THE ASSERTION. `…-decl-order-swapped/` is this same
-- program with the two modules' names exchanged and NOTHING else, and it ACCEPTED at
-- 3 on that same base binary. A reordering that changes no program meaning changing
-- the program's answer is the defect stated as an experiment; the pair is what pins
-- it, and either fixture alone can pass a compiler that is still order-decided.
import shared.{Box(..), Opaque(..)}
import amod.{bar}
import zmod.{foo}

main = println (foo (Box Opaque) + bar (Box 1))
